<div class="controls" role="group" aria-label="Execution scenario"><button data-case="pass" aria-pressed="true">All targets match</button><button data-case="mismatch" aria-pressed="false">One result differs</button><button data-case="timeout" aria-pressed="false">Runner times out</button><button data-case="unsupported" aria-pressed="false">Capability missing</button></div>
<div id="case-detail" class="scenario" aria-live="polite"><h3>All targets match</h3><p>Every required call has a typed result, all comparisons pass, sessions are disposed, and the publisher commits sources and verification evidence.</p></div>
<p>Execution runs against the transformed IR snapshot that fed generation. An optional transform-equivalence check is a different comparison and must name its before/after units explicitly.</p>
</section><section id="contracts"><div class="section-no">04 / E2</div><h2>Small contracts with explicit ownership</h2>
<p>Use a portable execution package for value, invocation and comparison contracts. It depends on IR and Ion codecs, not filesystem/process APIs. Put the Scheme evaluator adapter in a child package so other evaluators need not depend on Scheme. The engine resolves registered components; the host owns their effects and lifetime.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Contract</th><th>Owned data and rules</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>InvocationSuite</code></td><td>Profile, unique suite/call IDs, source-unit binding, public FQName, ordered arguments, policy references and optional independent expected outcomes. Argument and result types derive from the selected public signature.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ExecutionValue</code></td><td>Unit, Bool, exact Int, Float64, exact Decimal, UTF-16 String/Char, List, Tuple, closed Record and named Constructor. No closure, host object or executable string crosses this boundary.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>InvocationAdapter</code></td><td>Public export manifest, generated driver and codecs, dependency requirements and artifact membership. Export IDs refer to source FQNames and generated symbol identities, not handwritten names.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ExecutionPlan</code></td><td>Frozen unit and suite identities; selected generator, adapter, build provider, runtimes and evaluator; codecs; target/profile/build-mode matrix; per-call and total limits; publication policy.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ExecutableArtifact</code></td><td>Opaque host-owned handle, lease identity, target/backend, entry ABI, artifact digest, platform/architecture and build evidence. A supplied path alone is never build evidence.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ExecutionProvider</code></td><td>Describe capabilities, acquire a session, prepare a compiled artifact, invoke ordered calls, dispose. No engine-specific MoonBit manifest interpretation.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>EvaluatorProvider</code></td><td>Evaluate the same typed IR and calls with a named implementation, semantic profile, limits and identity. Unsupported input is explicit.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>CallOutcome</code></td><td>Either typed value, mapped model error, or execution failure. A matching domain error can satisfy a case; a timeout, crash, missing reply or malformed reply cannot.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ComparisonReport</code></td><td>Per-call/per-target outcome, evaluator outcome, policy, mismatch path, completion and parity status. Keep actual values and causal diagnostics.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Registrations select implementations by contract/profile/capabilities. A custom in-memory runner must pass the same contract tests. Process-based MoonBit is the first provider. The existing embedded package compiler remains a distinct capability; it does not become a workspace resolver by registration alone.</p>
</section><section id="values"><div class="section-no">05 / E2</div><h2>Types survive every boundary</h2>
<p>The invocation manifest resolves aliases and verifies public visibility, ordered arity, concrete type arguments and finite wire shapes before compilation. Callers cannot replace the declared type with a permissive tag. Validate records for missing, extra and duplicate fields; validate constructor ownership and arity. Enforce depth, element and byte limits before recursive processing.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Representation and comparison</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Int</td><td>Arbitrary precision integer. Ion int; JSON adapter uses a canonical signed digit string. Compare numerical value without a Double conversion.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decimal</td><td>Exact signed coefficient and exponent. Preserve representation in evidence; default numeric comparison ignores trailing zero scale. A scale-sensitive policy is separately named.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Float64</td><td>Preserve IEEE-754 bits, including signed zero and non-finite values. Default finite comparison is exact, signed zero distinct; NaNs compare by class, with raw bits retained. Approximate absolute/relative tolerance must be explicitly selected for transcendental cases, with no hidden epsilon.</td></tr>
<tr><td>String and Char</td><td>Preserve SDK UTF-16 code units, including isolated surrogates. Use an annotated code-unit list where Unicode text cannot represent the value. Char follows the SDK character invariant and is checked by its constructor.</td></tr>
<tr><td>List and Tuple</td><td>Ordered elements, fixed tuple arity, recursively typed. Unit is distinct from an empty list or an absent value.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Record</td><td>Unique canonical field identities; compare by field name and type. Wire field order is not a semantic difference. Unknown metadata fields are retained outside the semantic record.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Constructor</td><td>Type-qualified constructor identity and ordered payloads. Includes Maybe and Result. A model's <code>Result.Err</code> is a value, distinct from an SDK exception.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Functions</td><td>Internal closures and higher-order calls remain supported by E1. Boundary function arguments/results are refused in the initial profile. Curried returns may be fully applied by explicitly typed application arguments until a data result remains.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Generic entry points, open records, unresolved external implementations and unsupported SDK types fail capability validation. E2 does not expand the entire generator language surface as an incidental prerequisite.</p>
</section><section id="ion"><div class="section-no">06 / E2</div><h2>Ion profiles and JSON interoperability</h2>
<p>Version invocation suites, outcomes and receipts separately from Morphir IR, Ion encoding and tool releases. Proposed profile IDs are <code>morphir-execution-suite-v1</code>, <code>morphir-execution-outcome-v1</code> and <code>morphir-execution-receipt-v1</code>. Use the already pinned <code>moonrockz/ion</code> codecs for text and binary boundaries.</p>
<pre><code>morphir_execution_suite::{
  profile: "morphir-execution-suite-v1",
  id: "pricing-cases",
  unit: "pricing",
  calls: [{
    id: "standard-quote",
    entry: "pricing:quotes#total",
    arguments: [
      record::{quantity: int::3, unit_price: decimal::12.50}
    ]
  }],
  metadata: {purpose: "cross-target acceptance"}
}</code></pre>
<p>This is illustrative profile syntax, not an existing executable CLI fixture. The eventual fixture must reference an actual public signature from the checked-in model.</p>
<p>Reserved schema fields reject duplicates, unknown semantic kinds, contradictory tags and unsupported versions. Unknown annotations/extensions stay in metadata with their order and rich Ion values. Semantic hashes use a versioned canonical typed encoding; they do not hash incidental Ion symbol-table layout. Also retain raw input digests when byte-for-byte provenance matters.</p>
<p>In-process stages exchange typed values without serializing at every step. The process driver reads a bounded Ion invocation file and writes an Ion result file. The Node supervisor passes paths/handles and captures bounded diagnostics. A JSON-only provider uses a registered projection with strings for exact integers/decimals and explicit tags for Float64 bits and UTF-16 units. Round-trip checks prove that projection before it is accepted.</p>
<p>Existing E1 JSON control messages can remain a declared compatibility adapter. New machine intermediates and evidence default to binary Ion; authors can choose readable Ion text or a supported JSON profile. Global <code>--json</code> and <code>--json-lines</code> affect CLI reporting independently.</p>
</section><section id="adapter"><div class="section-no">07 / E2</div><h2>Call the generated public library</h2>
<p>The portable MoonBit adapter generator consumes the same typed IR plus E1's generated symbol mapping. It creates a separate consumer package importing the generated library. It must use actual exported functions, record shapes and constructors, so private access cannot accidentally pass because the driver was placed inside the library package.</p>
<p>The generated driver dispatches a bounded export ID, decodes and checks each argument, calls the public accessor/function with its declared parameter order and explicit curried applications, then encodes the result. Invocation data is read at execution time; new argument values do not trigger source generation or compilation. Values are never pasted into source code.</p>
<p>The executable contract is a private driver ABI, initially file-based invocation/result paths supplied as arguments. Generated protocol support depends on explicitly supplied pinned Ion and required transitive packages in addition to the SDK/core. The dependency resolver must attest to those copies; it cannot inherit the E1 provider's SDK-only assumption.</p>
<p>Public type/field/constructor identities need a typed export description alongside today's value symbols. Generate it from the existing lowering context rather than reverse-engineering MoonBit text. Driver artifacts have their own digest; adding a driver must not change the library digest or E1 metadata.</p>
<p>Outputs are runnable target artifacts behind host handles. A future stable JS import API, Wasm export ABI, native C ABI or WIT component wrapper may implement the same invocation contract, but E2 does not claim those packaging contracts merely because its process driver works.</p>
</section><section id="targets"><div class="section-no">08 / E2</div><h2>Target and host are separate choices</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Logical target</th><th>Compiler target</th><th>First runner</th><th>Acceptance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Wasm GC</td><td><code>wasm-gc</code></td><td>Identified <code>moonrun</code> with the required imports.</td><td>Required model parity, debug and release.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Native C</td><td><code>native</code></td><td>Executable on a matching OS/architecture; record C compiler/linker identity.</td><td>Required model parity on supported CI host.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Native LLVM</td><td><code>llvm</code></td><td>Executable on a matching host; record backend/linker identity.</td><td>Required separate gate. Capability-probe the pinned toolchain early.</td></tr>
<tr><td>JavaScript</td><td><code>js</code></td><td>Identified Node process with a bounded lifetime.</td><td>Required model parity and installed npm path.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Linear-memory Wasm</td><td><code>wasm</code></td><td>Existing runner where available.</td><td>Keep E1 regression coverage; optional E2 extension.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Target support does not imply that the current host can run the output. Negotiate compile target, executable format, OS/architecture, runner imports, process interruption, protocol codecs and resource limits before effects. Unsupported required targets fail planning; no target is silently dropped or substituted.</p>
<div class="callout open"><strong>Initial target probe, 1 October 2026</strong><p>A minimal program returned 42 on Wasm GC, native C and JavaScript using the pinned installation. LLVM reported that the backend is experimental/nightly-only and failed because its core prelude interface was absent. A matching private core build or explicit toolchain capability decision is still required. This is feasibility evidence, not E2 model parity.</p></div><p>Begin with acquired process tooling. Browser and non-browser embedded providers remain pluggable, but complete project resolution, dependency loading and asynchronous engine bridging require their own implementation and tests. Process isolation provides interruption and cleanup; it is not a security sandbox.</p>
</section><section id="lifecycle"><div class="section-no">09 / E2</div><h2>Keep artifacts alive only while needed</h2><figure class="diagram" id="e2-lifecycle"><h3>Execution session lifecycle</h3><div class="diagram-render" aria-label="Execution session lifecycle"><svg id="e2-lifecycle-svg" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width: 864.5px;" viewBox="-60.5 -10 864.5 1087" role="graphics-document document" aria-roledescription="sequence"><g><rect x="604" y="962" fill="#eaeaea" stroke="#666" width="150" height="74" name="P" rx="6" ry="6" class="actor actor-bottom" data-look="neo" filter="url(#e2-lifecycle-svg-drop-shadow)"></rect><text x="679" y="999" dominant-baseline="central" alignment-baseline="central" class="actor actor-box" style="text-anchor: middle; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;"><tspan x="679" dy="0">Publisher</tspan></text></g><g><rect x="404" y="962" fill="#eaeaea" stroke="#666" width="150" height="74" name="R" rx="6" ry="6" class="actor actor-bottom" data-look="neo" filter="url(#e2-lifecycle-svg-drop-shadow)"></rect><text x="479" y="999" dominant-baseline="central" alignment-baseline="central" class="actor actor-box" style="text-anchor: middle; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;"><tspan x="479" dy="0">Target runner</tspan></text></g><g><rect x="202" y="962" fill="#eaeaea" stroke="#666" width="150" height="74" name="S" rx="6" ry="6" class="actor actor-bottom" data-look="neo" filter="url(#e2-lifecycle-svg-drop-shadow)"></rect><text x="277" y="999" dominant-baseline="central" alignment-baseline="central" class="actor actor-box" style="text-anchor: middle; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;"><tspan x="277" dy="0">Execution session</tspan></text></g><g><rect x="0" y="962" fill="#eaeaea" stroke="#666" width="150" height="74" name="H" rx="6" ry="6" class="actor actor-bottom" data-look="neo" filter="url(#e2-lifecycle-svg-drop-sh