-et="message" data-id="i13" data-from="H" data-to="H" stroke-width="2" stroke="none" marker-end="url(#e2-lifecycle-svg-arrowhead)" style="fill: none;"></path></svg></div><figcaption>E1 build-only callers use prepare/build/dispose. E2 callers retain the session through execution. Sessions never leak private file paths as durable executable identities.</figcaption><details><summary>Mermaid source</summary><pre><code class="mermaid-source">sequenceDiagram
  participant H as Host orchestrator
  participant S as Execution session
  participant R as Target runner
  participant P as Publisher
  H-&gt;&gt;S: Acquire frozen model, adapter, dependencies
  H-&gt;&gt;S: Build requested target
  S--&gt;&gt;H: Executable handle + build evidence
  H-&gt;&gt;S: Invoke suite with deadline and limits
  S-&gt;&gt;R: Run verified artifact with typed calls
  R--&gt;&gt;S: Bounded result + diagnostics
  S--&gt;&gt;H: Correlated typed outcomes
  H-&gt;&gt;H: Compare evaluator and target outcomes
  H-&gt;&gt;S: Dispose and await cleanup
  alt All required cases match and cleanup succeeds
    H-&gt;&gt;P: Stage sources + Ion verification evidence
    P--&gt;&gt;H: Actual committed destinations
  else Mismatch, failure, cancellation or cleanup error
    H-&gt;&gt;H: Report failed attempt and preserve prior publication
  end
</code></pre></details></figure>
<p>Session states are acquired → prepared → built → running → completed → disposed, with failed/cancelled transitions into disposal from every effectful state. Reject invoking before build, use after disposal, reused lease IDs and replies for another artifact, target, suite or call.</p>
<p>Use fresh compiler output directories and private result paths. Hash compiled artifacts before invocation and verify identity after execution. Bound input bytes, result bytes, diagnostics, number/depth of values, per-call time, overall time and evaluator fuel. Deadlines include queue/build/run time where applicable.</p>
<p>Cancel child process trees and await termination before removing their workspace. Handle Unix process groups and Windows process-tree semantics explicitly; direct-child termination alone is insufficient. Aggregate cleanup errors with the original cause. A killed process, stale file or partial report never becomes a successful outcome.</p>
<p>No compilation, run or evaluator call occurs while holding the publication lock. Ordinary invocation returns outcomes without replacing E1 output. Verification with explicit publication commits the entire accepted task; failed attempt diagnostics remain available to the caller without replacing previously verified evidence.</p>
</section><section id="oracle"><div class="section-no">10 / E2</div><h2>Define what agreement proves</h2>
<div class="grid2"><div class="panel"><h3>Local evaluator lane</h3><p>Use <code>morphir-scheme/backend.run_file</code> with runtime native accelerators disabled for the acceptance model. It evaluates IR through a different path from MoonBit generation. Convert values through an independently tested typed adapter.</p><p>Scheme and generated code share parts of the SDK. Agreement checks translation and execution, but cannot alone prove that a shared SDK rule is correct.</p></div><div class="panel"><h3>Independent conformance lane</h3><p>Use pinned independently derived expected cases and, where a runnable implementation is available, a pinned Rust <code>morphir-runtime</code> provider. Record exact supported types, IR versions and error mappings. Compare only declared intersections.</p><p>The inspected Rust checkout is a placeholder. The umbrella's draft evaluator contract exposes a narrower tagged JSON value set. Establish a runnable revision and lossless IR adapter before claiming a Rust differential gate.</p></div></div>
<p>Core E2 completion requires a live local evaluator on the same representative model and all required targets, plus independent expected cases for sensitive semantics. Rust parity is a separate reported conformance lane until its provider is proven; an unavailable lane is explicitly unsupported and never displayed as passed. If roadmap acceptance requires Rust specifically, that lane becomes a required gate and E2 remains open until it runs.</p>
<p>Start with exact arithmetic, argument order, captured variables, lazy boolean branches, record updates, constructor payloads, collection callbacks and division-by-zero behavior. Add Float64 signed zero/non-finite cases and UTF-16 boundaries. Inject an incorrect result deliberately to show that the comparator detects a real semantic difference.</p>
<p>Normalize model errors through explicit enumerated mappings at the adapter boundary. Do not classify an arbitrary exception by matching a fragment of its text. Unknown exceptions are provider failures. Preserve original diagnostic text separately from the stable code used for comparison.</p>
</section><section id="cli"><div class="section-no">11 / E2</div><h2>Make execution visible in the engine and CLI</h2>
<p>Proposed additive commands below belong to this repository's <code>apps/morphir</code> CLI. They are design examples, not commands implemented by E1.</p>
<pre><code>morphir execute . --suite cases.ion --target js   --execution-provider process --home /tools/moon --sdk /sources/sdk

morphir verify . --suite cases.ion --target wasm-gc   --target native --target llvm --target js --evaluator scheme   --execution-provider process --home /tools/moon --sdk /sources/sdk   --json-lines

morphir verify . --suite cases.ion --dry-run --json</code></pre>
<p><code>execute</code> returns model outcomes for selected targets. <code>verify</code> additionally evaluates and compares; any mismatch or incomplete required case returns a nonzero status. Model errors count as matched only under the case's declared expected-error policy. Unknown options, contradictory target choices and missing providers fail before generation.</p>
<p>Configuration names registered providers/codecs/policies, dependency sources, default target matrix and limits. CLI options override those values using the existing workspace precedence rules. Dry run shows planned calls, required capabilities and target/evaluator coverage without acquiring tools, building, running or publishing.</p>
<p>Keep invocation arguments inside the suite file, so shell parsing cannot change exact values. JSON/JSON Lines reports carry exact values through the explicit tagged projection, preserve one terminal status per call/target, and distinguish <code>built</code>, <code>executed</code>, <code>compared</code> and <code>published</code>. Event order is stable even if a later provider supports concurrency.</p>
<p>An empty selected suite fails verification. Duplicate calls/targets, missing/extra result IDs and unsupported cases are errors. The target matrix must be complete; reporting three successes out of four required targets is incomplete, not a pass.</p>
</section><section id="evidence"><div class="section-no">12 / E2</div><h2>Evidence that can be checked later</h2>
<p>An Ion execution receipt records the schema/profile version; original and normalized model identities; suite and call identities; generated library/export-manifest/driver identities; compiler, core and every dependency identity; executable digests; runtime versions/binary identities and platform; evaluator identity/profile; build mode; comparison policy; outcomes; limit settings and actual completion status.</p>
<p>Retain typed actual and evaluator results, expected values where supplied, and precise mismatch paths such as <code>calls[quote].result.total</code>. Error evidence includes call ID, public entry, stage and source-node identity. Do not treat provider-supplied <code>successful: true</code> as sufficient evidence; the orchestrator validates completeness, correlation and identities itself.</p>
<p>Semantic reuse keys include every behavior-relevant identity. Timing, temporary paths and diagnostic ordering stay outside semantic hashes. E2 starts without cross-run executable/result caching; add caching only after these identities and invalidation rules are exercised. This leaves room for Ion binary and symbol-table optimizations without making byte layout part of semantic equality.</p>
</section><section id="delivery"><div class="section-no">13 / E2</div><h2>Deliver complete paths in six slices</h2>
<p>All implementation slices are agent-executable within this design. A slice is complete only with its real end-to-end evidence; mark a target or oracle unsupported when feasibility has not been established.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Slice</th><th>Demonstrable outcome</th><th>Dependencies</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1. Scalar JS invocation</td><td>Ion/JSON suite → generated public driver → JS process → typed result → Scheme comparison → CLI report. Establish target/oracle feasibility evidence.</td><td>E1</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Wasm GC and native execution</td><td>The same scalar suite runs on Wasm GC, native C and LLVM with distinct runtime/build identities and explicit capability failures.</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Representative pricing parity</td><td>Exact numbers, records, constructors, collections and model errors survive the boundary and agree for the same model/calls across all required targets.</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Failure-safe verification</td><td>Timeouts, cancellation, crashes, malformed/stale replies and cleanup failures are reported accurately while prior published output survives.</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Independent conformance evidence</td><td>Sensitive semantic cases have independent expectations; a proven pinned Rust lane runs its supported intersection or records its explicit unavailable boundary.</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>6. Installed CLI and CI gate</td><td>Clean-checkout/installed-package execution, JSON and Ion sources, debug/release target matrix and complete reports pass the repository gates.</td><td>4, 5</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Plans and execution ledgers live in shared beads under <code>morphir-ukvn.5.3</code>. This HTML remains local under <code>.dev/</code>, with its canonical design stored on that bead. Implementation changes go on <code>DamianReeves/morphir-e2-execution</code>, based on the E1 squash merge.</p>
</section><section id="decisions"><div class="section-no">14 / E2</div><h2>Defaults and boundaries to keep explicit</h2>
<div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D1</div><div><h3>Use a separate public consumer driver</h3><p>It proves exported access and keeps library generation reusable. The first ABI is an internal Ion file protocol; stable host-language library ABIs remain separate capabilities.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D2</div><div><h3>Keep build, execution, comparison and publication distinct</h3><p>Reuse E1 preparation/identity rules through a session. Build-only callers dispose immediately; execution callers retain the verified artifact until their calls finish.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D3</div><div><h3>Use Scheme for the always-available live evaluator</h3><p>Record shared SDK limitations and independently derived cases. Rust support needs a proven pin; do not silently make an unavailable external checkout a required default for ordinary users.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D4</div><div><h3>Include LLVM explicitly</h3><p>The roadmap names C and LLVM. Treat them as separate native backends and prove availability early. A missing backend needs a documented scope decision, never an automatic downgrade.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision"><div class="decision-id">D5</div><div><h3>Preserve rich values with Ion</h3><p>Use typed in-process contracts, binary Ion intermediates and explicit JSON projection where required. Preserve exact numerics, annotations and UTF-16 semantics.</p></div></div>
<div class="callout open"><strong>What E2 completion does not claim</strong><p>Full generic IR/SDK coverage; stable C/JS/WIT library packaging; multi-document linking; embedded project resolution; browser parity; upstream Rust CLI extension integration; or a published release. Each needs its own acceptance evidence.</p></div>
</section><section id="sources"><div class="section-no">15 / E2</div><h2>Implementation references</h2>
<ul class="refs">
<li><a href="../../pkgs/morphir-moonbit/README.md">E1 generator scope and output contracts</a></li>
<li><a href="../../pkgs/morphir-moonbit/types.mbt">Generator project and symbol types</a></li>
